// XFAIL: DF-226e a bare literal in a `Result` slot never adopts the payload's
// fixed width, so an `Int32` Ok (or Err) payload is an internal compiler error
//
// The sibling of DF-226d, found by the same sweep and left unfixed because it
// needs a ruling the optional case does not. `_apply_literal_expected_type`
// peels an OPTIONAL expectation to its payload — there is exactly one payload,
// so nothing is guessed — but a `Result` has TWO, and which one an untyped
// literal means is only obvious when just one of them is a fixed-width integer:
//
//   `Result<Int32, Bad>`   + `return 4`  -> Ok(4), the only reading
//   `Result<String, Int32>` + `return 4` -> Err(4), the only reading
//   `Result<Int32, Int8>`  + `return 4`  -> AMBIGUOUS; both payloads could
//                                           adopt it, and picking one silently
//                                           decides whose reading the program
//                                           runs under
//
// The third row is why this is filed rather than fixed: the rule wanted is
// probably "peel to the unique payload that could adopt it, and refuse the
// ambiguity with a clean error naming both" — which is a design ruling, and the
// language already treats `T == E` as a compile error for the neighbouring
// reason. Today all three are `ResultOkWrap`/`ResultErrWrap` codegen ICEs.
//
// A named function and a closure body have this identically (the closure has
// reached the same funnel since DF-226a), so it is the funnel's gap, not a
// caller's. Workaround meanwhile: write the suffix (`return 4i32`).
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// ok = 4
// err = 7

struct Bad { code: Int }
extension Bad: Printable {
    func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) { into.append("bad") }
}
extension Bad: Error {}

func ok_side() -> Result<Int32, Bad> { return 4 }

func err_side() -> Result<String, Int32> { return 7 }

func main() {
    match ok_side() {
        case Ok(v) -> print("ok = {v}"),
        case Err(_) -> print("ok = err")
    }
    match err_side() {
        case Ok(s) -> print("err = {s}"),
        case Err(e) -> print("err = {e}")
    }
}
